            <div class="space-y-4">
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What causes extremely loud snoring?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Loud snoring is caused by significant airway narrowing, often due to enlarged tonsils, excess soft tissue, nasal obstruction, or anatomical factors. The louder the snoring, the more severe the obstruction tends to be.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Is loud snoring a sign of sleep apnea?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Very loud snoring, especially with gasping sounds and daytime fatigue, is often associated with sleep apnea. A sleep study can determine if sleep apnea is present.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can loud snoring be cured permanently?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Surgical treatment can permanently reduce or eliminate snoring by addressing the anatomical cause. However, some patients may need lifestyle changes or ongoing use of oral appliances.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How long does recovery from snoring surgery take?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Minimally invasive procedures like radiofrequency ablation require 1-2 weeks recovery. Traditional soft palate surgery requires 2-3 weeks with diet restrictions.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Do snoring mouthpieces really work?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Custom-fitted oral appliances are effective for many patients with mild to moderate snoring. They work by advancing the jaw to keep airways more open during sleep.</div></details>
